rtune Global 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 84, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 39, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 83, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 30, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "aiq", "title": "Fortune AIQ 50", "year": 2025, "rank": 45, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 93, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 40, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 96, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 33, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 111, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--financials", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Financials", "year": 2024, "rank": 10, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 41, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 36, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 40, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 200, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 36, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": "The investment bank and financial services company saw modest revenue growth in 2022, but its profits slumped as dealmaking dried up in a rocky economy.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>The bank reported $65.9 billion in revenue in 2022, up 8% from the year prior.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Morgan Stanley\u2019s profits dipped 27% to $11 billion as the firm\u2019s investment banking arm struggled amid high inflation and a scarcity of M&amp;A deals.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>After some layoffs in 2022, the firm is reportedly planning more job cuts\u20143,000 or so\u2014for 2023, with its banking and trading units enduring most of the reductions.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>James Gorman, the company\u2019s CEO since 2010, announced in May that he would step down within the next 12 months. The company did not immediately name a successor.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 211, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Banks had a great 2021, and Morgan Stanley was no exception. The bank posted record net revenues (up 23%) and net income (up 37%) for the year, and its wealth management division added $1 trillion to client assets. Morgan Stanley also advised on several significant and big-ticket mergers this year, including Canada Pacific Railway\u2019s $31 billion acquisition of Kansas City Southern to help create a Canada-U.S.-Mexico rail network. (The deal\u2019s final approval was pending regulatory review as of May 2022.) The rate of mergers and acquisitions dropped significantly in the first few months of 2022, however, as geopolitical concerns and inflation left investors and corporate leaders feeling skittish.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 204, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;What pandemic? Morgan Stanley appeared to weather the shutdowns seamlessly, even though CEO James Gorman was diagnosed with the coronavirus in March 2020. Boosted by surging capital markets (and without a consumer business to weigh it down during the lockdowns), revenue rose 16% to $48.2 million. The strong results seem validation of the bank\u2019s decisions to spend billions of dollars doubling down on its brokerage and wealth management segments: In 2020, the bank acquired digital brokerage E*Trade for $13 billion and announced plans to acquire investment management firm Eaton Vance for $7 billion. The company\u2019s upward trajectory hasn\u2019t exactly been consistent, however. The bank lost about $911 million in the first quarter of 2021 owing to the collapse of family office Archegos Capital Management. While the firm still posted a record profit for the quarter, the incident is likely to present further challenges as regulators prod why financiers (including Morgan Stanley) had so much riding on a single client. \\n&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">What pandemic? Morgan Stanley appeared to weather the shutdowns seamlessly, even though CEO James Gorman was diagnosed with the coronavirus in March 2020. Boosted by surging capital markets (and without a consumer business to weigh it down during the lockdowns), revenue rose 16% to $48.2 million. The strong results seem validation of the bank\u2019s decisions to spend billions of dollars doubling down on its brokerage and wealth management segments: In 2020, the bank acquired digital brokerage E*Trade for $13 billion and announced plans to acquire investment management firm Eaton Vance for $7 billion. The company\u2019s upward trajectory hasn\u2019t exactly been consistent, however. The bank lost about $911 million in the first quarter of 2021 owing to the collapse of family office Archegos Capital Management. While the firm still posted a record profit for the quarter, the incident is likely to present further challenges as regulators prod why financiers (including Morgan Stanley) had so much riding on a single client.<br />\r\n</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;What pandemic? Morgan Stanley appeared to weather the shutdowns seamlessly, even though CEO James Gorman was diagnosed with the coronavirus in March 2020. Boosted by surging capital markets (and without a consumer business to weigh it down during the lockdowns), revenue rose 16% to $48.2 million. The strong results seem validation of the bank\u2019s decisions to spend billions of dollars doubling down on its brokerage and wealth management segments: In 2020, the bank acquired digital brokerage E*Trade for $13 billion and announced plans to acquire investment management firm Eaton Vance for $7 billion. The company\u2019s upward trajectory hasn\u2019t exactly been consistent, however. The bank lost about $911 million in the first quarter of 2021 owing to the collapse of family office Archegos Capital Management. While the firm still posted a record profit for the quarter, the incident is likely to present further challenges as regulators prod why financiers (including Morgan Stanley) had so much riding on a single client. \\n&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">What pandemic? Morgan Stanley appeared to weather the shutdowns seamlessly, even though CEO James Gorman was diagnosed with the coronavirus in March 2020. Boosted by surging capital markets (and without a consumer business to weigh it down during the lockdowns), revenue rose 16% to $48.2 million. The strong results seem validation of the bank\u2019s decisions to spend billions of dollars doubling down on its brokerage and wealth management segments: In 2020, the bank acquired digital brokerage E*Trade for $13 billion and announced plans to acquire investment management firm Eaton Vance for $7 billion. The company\u2019s upward trajectory hasn\u2019t exactly been consistent, however. The bank lost about $911 million in the first quarter of 2021 owing to the collapse of family office Archegos Capital Management. While the firm still posted a record profit for the quarter, the incident is likely to present further challenges as regulators prod why financiers (including Morgan Stanley) had so much riding on a single client.<br />\r\n</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 203, "itemDescription": "Since the 2008 financial crisis, Morgan Stanley has increasingly leaned on its wealth management business as a steadier revenue source than trading or investment banking. Morgan Stanley has doubled down on the strategy with success: Last year wealth and investment management grew to 51% of the company's revenue, compared to 26% in 2010. Now the bank is spending billions to capture a younger, more tech-savvy clientele. In February 2019, Morgan Stanley acquired Solium Capital, which helps manage stock compensation plans for the likes of Instacart, Stripe, and Shopify, for $900 million. In early 2020, the bank titan made an even larger play for the masses by acquiring discount brokerage E*Trade Financial for $13 billion\u2014putting it head-to-head with the likes of Charles Schwab and Robinhood."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": "Since the 2008 financial crisis, Morgan Stanley has increasingly leaned on its wealth management business as a steadier revenue source, rather than on trading and investment banking. Morgan Stanley has doubled down on the strategy with success: Last year wealth and investment management grew to 51% of the company's revenue, compared to 26% in 2010. Now, the bank is spending billions to capture a younger, more tech-savvy clientele. In February 2019, Morgan Stanley acquired Solium Capital, which helps manage stock compensation plans for the likes of Instacart, Stripe, and Shopify, for $900 million. In early 2020, the bank titan made an even larger play for the masses by acquiring discount brokerage E*Trade Financial for $13 billion\u2014putting it head-to-head with the likes of Charles Schwab and Robinhood."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 218, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 63, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 249, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 67, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 267, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 76, "it